2025_sustainability_report_0478ad88.pdf::chunk-24553,2025_sustainability_report_0478ad88.pdf,0478ad88's 2025 Sustainability Report,ENGIE,utilities_energy,2025,clean_esg_or_sustainability_report,15,17,chunk-24553,8,,environmental,False,True,"range represents the maximum level of emissions that the Group undertakes not to exceed. (1) Mainly electricity and gas (2) Mainly gas ENVIRONMENT CLIMATE NATURE GENERAL INFORMATION SOCIAL SOCIETAL GOVERNANCE 2024 ESG AT ENGIE 16 MOODY’S ASSESSMENT NZ-2 In February 2024, Moody’s assessed ENGIE's transition plan with a rating of Ambition: 1.5°C Implementation: “solid” level A summary of the assessment is available below along with the full report directly on the Moody's website: (http://www.moodys.com/researchdocumentcontentpage.aspx?docid=PBC_1388307) This assessment is based on the prior objectives of the Group's climate strategy ENVIRONMENT CLIMATE NATURE GENERAL INFORMATION SOCIAL SOCIETAL GOVERNANCE 2024 ESG AT ENGIE Performance indicators Adjustments Safeguards Light Green - Confirmed 17 SUSTAINABLE FITCH ASSESSMENT The outcome of Sustainable Fitch’s Transition Assessment for Engie S.A. is ‘Light Green -’, indicating an advanced transition plan featuring ambitious and largely comprehensive long- term and interim targets, including net-zero absolute Scopes 1, 2 and 3 emissions by 2045. These are backed by a credible business transformation plan to steadily reduce the share of fossil fuel-based activities and products in Engie’s business mix and ramp up investment in green technologies such as wind and solar. Engie has a strong track record implementing its transition plan. Its total carbon footprint declined by 40% since 2017, driven largely by the declining fossil fuel-based generation as a share of total installed capacity, while a small but material share of its revenue now comes from transition-related products and services. ENGIE’s investment decisions are consistent with its climate goals, with the largest capex allocated to greening or decarbonizing activities in 2023. ENGIE-TRANSITION ASSESSMENT PROCESS ENGIE’S TRANSITION PLAN AND PATHWAY – STRENGTHS AND WEAKNESSES - No firm commitment to phase out fossil fuel-based generation - Some material categories not covered in interim Scope 3 targets + Substantial investments in Greenland decarbonizing activities Renewables account for a rising share of production capacity and revenue + Comprehensive long-term targets aiming for net zero by 2045 Overall, a solid track record of GHG emissions reductions + + Light Green Outcome 1 2 3 Assessment steps TRANSITION SPECTRUM Black Brown Light Brown Olive Light Green Green - = + Scale Note: The assessments builds on a transition spectrum, offering extra granularity with additional sub-stages to each colour stage, such as “-”, “=“, “+”. Source: Sustainable Fitch This assessment, as of December 2024, is therefore based on the prior objectives of the Group's climate strategy ENVIRONMENT CLIMATE NATURE GENERAL INFORMATION SOCIAL SOCIETAL GOVERNANCE"
