2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39373,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,17,18,chunk-39373,15,,social,False,False,"– Other liabilities 412 374 38 38 – of which taxes 133 133 0 0 – of which social security 42 42 – – – 125,597 53,995 71,602 62,737 8,865 18 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements TERM TO MATURITY € million Dec. 31, 2024 due within one year due in more than one year OF WHICH DUE WITHIN ONE TO FIVE YEARS OF WHICH DUE OVER 5 YEARS Type of liability Liabilities to banks 2,481 224 2,257 1,757 500 Payments received on account of orders 317 317 – – – Trade payables 2,694 2,694 – – – Liabilities to affiliated companies 114,900 55,814 59,086 46,511 12,576 Liabilities to other investees and investors 1,330 1,330 – – – Other liabilities 926 863 62 62 – of which taxes 152 152 0 0 – of which social security 43 43 – – – 122,648 61,243 61,406 48,330 13,076 Volkswagen AG’s syndicated credit line of €12.5 billion agreed in March 2025 was unused at the end of 2025. In May 2025, Volkswagen AG called a hybrid note (maturity: 5 years) with a principal amount of €1,500 million, which had been placed in 2020 via Volkswagen International Finance N.V., Amsterdam/the Netherlands (issuer). The hybrid note was redeemed on June 17, 2025. From the hybrid capital issued on May 15, 2025, Volkswagen AG recorded a cash inflow of €1,900 million. Volkswagen Group of America Finance, LLC, Herndon placed bonds with a total volume of USD 5.5 billion on the US capital market in March and September 2025. In November 2025, a note was issued in the Canadian market in three tranches with a total volume of CAD 1 billion. After Volkswagen International Finance N.V. had already been active locally on the Chinese capital market in previous years, it again issued what are known as Panda bonds worth CNY 2.5 billion in May and November 2025. In addition, Volkswagen International Finance N.V. issued senior, unsecured bonds with a total volume of €0.9 billion in May and August 2025 based on the Green Finance Framework. €4.9 billion (previous year: €4.9 billion) of the liabilities to affiliated companies relates to trade payables. The increase in liabilities to affiliated companies is mainly the result of higher cash pool and loan liabilities. Liabilities from loss absorption declined year-on-year. The liabilities to other investees and investors contain trade payables of €180 million (previous year: €143 million). Other liabilities include option premiums in the amount of €71 million (previous year: €122 million). Standard retention of title applies to the liabilities from deliveries of goods contained in the amounts shown above. The recognition of trade payables resulted in prior-period cost of materials of €0.4 billion in the fiscal year. Other liabilities include liabilities to employees of €0.0 million (previous year: €0.0 million) that are secured by real estate liens."
2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39374,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,18,19,chunk-39374,16,,,False,True,"cost of materials of €0.4 billion in the fiscal year. Other liabilities include liabilities to employees of €0.0 million (previous year: €0.0 million) that are secured by real estate liens. 19 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements (11) DEFERRED INCOME Deferred income primarily comprises amounts for extended warranties and fees collected in relation to online services (Car-Net). CONTINGENCIES AND COMMITMENTS € million Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Contingent liabilities from guarantees 233 226 Contingent liabilities from warranties 43,963 47,407 of which relating to pensions 968 865 of which relating to affiliated companies 968 865 Granting of security for third-party liabilities 2,391 2,378 of which relating to affiliated companies 2,097 2,291 46,587 50,011 CONTINGENT LIABILITIES FROM WARRANTIES Contingent liabilities from warranties relate primarily to guarantees given to creditors of subsidiaries and for bonds issued by these subsidiaries. RISK ASSESSMENT OF THE SETTLEMENT OF CONTINGENT LIABILITIES Volkswagen AG provides guarantees for the capital market issues of the finance companies, for development loans from supranational financial institutions and, in specific cases, for loans to newly formed subsidiaries. Volkswagen AG manages its subsidiaries in such a way that they can discharge their financial obligations at any time. In addition to the preparation of a monthly liquidity report for Volkswagen AG, regular financial reviews are held during which the variances between the actual and projected liquidity are analyzed and the necessary corrective measures are implemented. Based on this information, the Company sees no risk of a claim being brought under the guarantees provided. For more information, please refer to the disclosures on existing contingent liabilities in the section entitled “Related party disclosures”. TRANSACTIONS NOT INCLUDED IN THE BALANCE SHEET (SECTION 285 NO. 3 OF THE HGB) Volkswagen AG financed some of its trade receivables from selected non-Group importers on the basis of nonrecourse factoring via foreign subsidiaries. In addition, selected receivables from partners of the domestic sales organization are financed on the basis of non-recourse factoring via a domestic subsidiary. The total amount concerned was €15.6 billion (previous year: €15.6 billion) in the fiscal year. The Company received liquid funds in this amount. These transactions do not lead to any specific new risks. Volkswagen AG sells a small number of vehicles, mainly to car rental companies, subject to the obligation to repurchase them for a predefined price after a fixed period of time. As of December 31, 2025, this was the case for 20,369 vehicles worth €0.4 billion (previous year: 15,545 vehicles worth €0.3 billion). Provisions are recognized for the risk arising from potential differences between the agreed prices and the market prices when such vehicles are marketed in the future."
2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39375,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,19,20,chunk-39375,17,,governance,False,False,"vehicles worth €0.3 billion). Provisions are recognized for the risk arising from potential differences between the agreed prices and the market prices when such vehicles are marketed in the future. 20 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements TOTAL FEES PAID TO AUDITORS The total fee paid to the auditors of the consolidated financial statements in Germany can be found in Note 41 of the notes to the consolidated financial statements. The financial statement audit services related to the audit of the consolidated financial statements of Volkswagen AG and to the annual financial statements of subsidiaries, as well as to reviews of the interim consolidated financial statements of Volkswagen AG and of the interim financial statements of subsidiaries. Other assurance services mainly related to statutory and non-statutory audits, and to non-statutory assurance services, including the audit of substainability reports. OTHER FINANCIAL OBLIGATIONS € million Dec. 31, 2025 Due 2026 Due 2027 - 2030 Due after 2030 Loan commitments 27,489 27,489 – – of which related to affiliated companies 27,475 27,475 – – Rental and leasing agreements 1,272 493 574 205 of which related to affiliated companies 408 266 85 56 Other Contracts 5,423 2,631 2,014 779 of which related to affiliated companies 1,935 1,180 714 41 of which related to associated companies 142 142 – – 34,184 30,612 2,588 984 The other financial obligations from long-term rental and leasing agreements comprise lease payments for business vehicles, rentals of storage, logistics and office space, test tracks as well as operating and office equipment. Around 44 hectares of land (carrying amount €7.1 million) are encumbered by heritable building rights. In accordance with Art. 5(10) of the statutes of the Einlagensicherungsfonds (Deposit Protection Fund), Volkswagen AG has given an undertaking to indemnify Bundesverband deutscher Banken e.V., Berlin, against any losses incurred that are attributable to measures taken by it in favor of a majority-owned bank. Volkswagen AG has liabilities from its investments in commercial partnerships. Other financial obligations relate mostly to obligations under development and supply contracts, as well as service agreements. In addition to the other financial obligations shown in the table, purchase commitments exist for inventories with a short turnover period, which arise primarily from the Master Collaboration Agreement with Ford Motor Company for the joint development of vans and mid-sized pickups for the global market. In addition, there are purchase commitments at normal levels for capital expenditure projects and advertising contracts."
2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39376,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,20,21,chunk-39376,18,,governance,False,False,"Company for the joint development of vans and mid-sized pickups for the global market. In addition, there are purchase commitments at normal levels for capital expenditure projects and advertising contracts. 21 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements Disclosures on derivatives MEASUREMENT METHODS The fair values of the derivatives generally correspond to the market or quoted market price. If no active market exists, fair value is determined using valuation techniques, such as by discounting the future cash flows at the market interest rate, or by using recognized option pricing models, and verified by confirmations from the banks that handle the transactions. The calculations were based on the following term structures: in % EUR CAD CHF CNY CZK GBP JPY PLN SEK USD Interest rate for six months 1.9340 2.2755 –0.0450 1.6096 3.3887 3.6467 0.7833 3.8537 2.0975 3.5690 Interest rate for one year 1.9390 2.3355 –0.0370 1.5842 3.4562 3.5406 0.9043 3.6145 2.1024 3.4125 Interest rate for five years 2.3274 2.7630 0.3195 1.7200 3.8303 3.6570 1.5043 3.7394 2.5580 3.4155 Interest rate for ten years 2.6780 3.1330 0.6640 1.8750 4.1210 3.9965 1.8783 4.1725 2.9350 3.7560 DERIVATIVES Currency forwards and swaps, currency options, commodity forwards and swaps, cross-currency interest rate swaps and interest rate swaps are used as hedging instruments. Derivatives used to hedge commodity price risks and the foreign currency hedges attributable to them are combined into valuation units. All instruments serve to hedge currency, interest rate and commodity price risk exposures of hedged items attributable to the real economy, independently of whether or not they are included in hedge accounting. In the fiscal year under review, existing hedges of sales revenues and purchase transactions were terminated because the hedged items no longer met the criteria for hedge accounting. In cases in which they were settled, new hedging relationships were designated for the currency forwards and commodity swaps concerned. The following table shows the hedging volume of the financial instruments not included in hedge accounting. NOTIONAL AMOUNT FAIR VALUE € million; type and volume Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Currency forwards contracts 1,770 1,539 of which currency purchases 852 756 of which positive fair values 2 8 of which negative fair values –2 –1 of which currency sales 918 783 of which positive fair values 1 1 of which negative fair values –1 –5 Currency option contracts – – of which positive fair values – – Commodity swaps contracts 45 99 of which positive fair values 6 6 of which negative fair values – –1"
2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39377,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,21,22,chunk-39377,19,,governance,False,False,"option contracts – – of which positive fair values – – Commodity swaps contracts 45 99 of which positive fair values 6 6 of which negative fair values – –1 22 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements BALANCE SHEET ITEMS AND CARRYING AMOUNTS Derivatives are contained in the following balance sheet items at the carrying amounts shown: CARRYING AMOUNT € million Balance sheet item Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Expected losses from open currency forwards Other provisions 27 39 Expected losses from open commodity swap contracts Other provisions 54 171 DERIVATIVES – INCLUDED IN HEDGE ACCOUNTING Explanations of the risks hedged, the hedging strategy and the highly probable forecast transactions are included in the management report. HEDGES OF CURRENCY, INTEREST RATE AND COMMODITY PRICE RISK EXPOSURES The following risk exposures are included in hedge accounting: NOTIONAL AMOUNT FAIR VALUE € million; hedged risks Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Dec. 31, 2025 Dec. 31, 2024 Currency and interest rate risk from assets, liabilities (Currency forwards and cross currency interest rate swaps contracts) 5,585 3,630 negative fair values –83 – positive fair values 16 130 Currency risk from forecast transactions/executory contracts Currency forwards contracts 112,728 152,033 negative fair values –2,932 –3.566 positive fair values 3,208 4,909 Currency option contracts 7,002 13,946 negative fair values –123 –189 positive fair values 127 174 Commodity swaps contracts 9,519 12,443 negative fair values –754 –790 positive fair values 1,208 524 A portfolio approach is used to hedge currency risk exposures, under which expected cash inflows and outflows in foreign currencies are offset in order to hedge the net position. Since the volume of the hedges is lower than the volume of the planned commodity purchases and sales, there is a strong presumption that the changes in cash flows from hedging instruments in the future will offset the effects relating to commodity purchases and sales. Furthermore, the extent of hedging decreases the later the commodity purchase or sale is planned within the planning period. All hedging relationships within Hedge Accounting are accounted for using the freeze method. The recognized hedges were effective. Assets and liabilities in a nominal amount of €5.6 billion are hedged in micro hedges; the term of the hedge is based on the term of the underlying transaction. As of the reporting date, the risk resulting from the hedging of liabilities was €83 million. The effectiveness of the hedge is assessed prospectively using the critical terms match method and retrospectively using the dollar offset method. Micro hedges and macro hedges are recognized for the forecast transactions. Their effectiveness is assessed prospectively using the critical terms match method and retrospectively using the dollar offset method. With respect to the hedging of forecast transactions and executory contracts using currency forwards, risk exposures in the amount of €87.8 billion are hedged by micro hedges and €24.9 billion by macro hedges."
2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf::chunk-39378,2025_annual_report_1b21309a.pdf,1b21309a's 2025 Annual Report,Volkswagen,automotive,2025,long_doc,22,23,chunk-39378,20,,governance,False,False,"the hedging of forecast transactions and executory contracts using currency forwards, risk exposures in the amount of €87.8 billion are hedged by micro hedges and €24.9 billion by macro hedges. 23 Annual Financial Statements of Volkswagen AG Notes to the annual financial statements Hedges of forecast transactions and executory contracts using currency options are hedged by micro hedges in the amount of €6.0 billion and by macro hedges in the amount of €1.0 billion. Hedges of forecast transactions and executory contracts as part of commodity swaps are hedged by micro hedges in the amount of €6.0 billion and by macro hedges in the amount of €3.5 billion. Planned commodity purchases in foreign currency and revenue from vehicle sales that are highly probable in the coming five years. Planned purchases in connection with the future electric vehicle strategy also relates to periods beyond this. Currency risk exposures relating to executory contracts are hedged by micro hedges. In addition to the derivatives used for hedging foreign currency and price risk, the Group held options and other derivatives in connection with fund investments at the reporting date with a notional amount of €19.1 billion. Credit default swaps, also in connection with fund investments, had a notional amount of €12.8 billion. HEDGING OF CURRENCY AND COMMODITY PRICE RISK EXPOSURES FOR SUBSIDIARIES Volkswagen AG combines the currency and purchase price risk exposures of certain subsidiaries with its own exposures as part of uniform planning in order to hedge them using currency forwards, currency options and commodity swaps with external partners. The notional amounts of the aggregate hedging transactions entered into by Volkswagen AG for forecast transactions and planned commodity purchases therefore also includes amounts attributable to subsidiaries included in the consolidated financial statements. They are allocated to subsidiaries either via hedging transactions between the subsidiary and Volkswagen AG that mirror the external hedging transactions, or by the subsidiary participating in the gain or loss when the hedging transaction is settled The term and method used to assess the effectiveness of hedging transactions entered into between Volkswagen AG and a subsidiary are the same as for external hedging transactions. Hedge accounting is applied only to micro hedges. The underlying is defined as the entire hedging transaction or a part of the hedging transaction entered into between Volkswagen AG and external partners."
